 <!-- Top Attractions -->
 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="attractions" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">🎯 Top Attractions</h2>

 <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">🏖️ Sanya — Where Most Tourists End Up</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6">Sanya is the classic tropical beach vacation — think palm trees, turquoise water, and resorts that actually feel like a splurge. But there's more here than just lounging by the pool.</p>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Yalong Bay</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The best stretch of beach on the island — that's not hype, it's just true. The sand is that fine white coral powder you usually only see in postcards, the water's clear enough to spot fish from shore, and the whole bay curves for 7 kilometers backed by green hills. Most of the resorts front the beach directly, so you can stumble from your room to the water in about 90 seconds. It's pricier than other Sanya beaches, but you get what you pay for.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Dadonghai Bay</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">This is where the local energy lives. Dadonghai is less polished than Yalong — cheaper hotels, more noise, better people-watching. The bay curves into a crescent shape with decent swimming, and the entire waterfront is lined with seafood restaurants where you pick your catch from tanks and they grill it on the spot. Evening is the best time here: the lights come on, the beach bars fire up, and the whole place hums.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Haitang Bay</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The quieter, more upscale cousin. Haitang Bay is where the Atlantis Sanya resort sits — think a massive water park, an aquarium with 10,000+ marine animals, and a dolphin interaction pool that actually feels more tasteful than you'd expect. The beach itself is wide and less crowded than Yalong. If you're into duty-free shopping, this is also where the big免税店 is.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wuzhizhou Island</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">A 20-minute ferry from the coast gets you to this small island with the best snorkeling in Hainan. The water around it is protected, so the coral is healthy and the fish don't scatter the moment you dip your mask in. Boat-in boat-out, you can cover the whole place in a half-day. Go early to beat the crowds — the ferries fill up fast by 10 AM.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Nanshan Temple</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The centerpiece is the 108-meter Guanyin statue standing in the sea — three faces, three directions, the tallest statue of its kind in the world. But what surprises most visitors is the scale of the whole complex: halls, pagodas, gardens spread over acres of manicured grounds. It's an active temple with real monks and real incense smoke, not just a tourist stop dressed up as one.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Yanoda Rainforest</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Jungle with a few upgrades: glass-bottom bridge suspended over the canopy, zip-line across a valley, and waterfall pools you can actually wade into. The hike itself is manageable (couple of hours) and there's a surprising variety of plant life — look for the giant banyan trees. It gets humid fast, so bring water and go early.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Luhuitou Park</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The hilltop park with the best sunset view over Sanya. There's a legend behind the name: a hunter chased a deer to the edge of a cliff, and the deer turned into a beautiful woman (Luhuitou literally means "deer turning its head"). Hike up or take the tram — either way you get a panoramic view that makes you understand why this spot has been sacred for so long.</p>
 </div>

 <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">🏛️ Haikou — The Laid-Back Capital</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6">Haikou isn't Sanya, and that's the point. Less beach, more culture. Fewer tourists, more real life. It's where you go when you want to understand Hainan beyond the resort brochure.</p>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Volcanic Geopark</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">An extinct volcano you can actually walk into — the crater is about 30 minutes from Haikou's city center. The path takes you down into the cone where you're surrounded by volcanic rock and tropical vegetation. There are lava tunnels nearby you can crawl through (bring a flashlight). A genuinely unique experience that most Hainan tourists miss because they never leave the Sanya resorts.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Qilou Old Street</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Arcaded buildings from the 1920s lining both sides of the street — a blend of Chinese, European, and Southeast Asian styles that exists because Hainan was a trade hub back when these things mattered. The arcades (called qilou, hence the name) shelter the sidewalks so you can stroll even in the rain. The architecture is genuinely unique to Hainan, and you'll see it nowhere else in China at this scale.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Hainan Museum</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The best place to learn about the Li and Miao minority cultures — two groups that lived on this island for centuries before the resorts arrived. The Li brocade collection (a UNESCO Intangible Heritage) is the highlight: intricate geometric patterns woven by hand using techniques that haven't changed in 3,000 years. You can also see the boat-shaped houses the Li used to live in, and artifacts from the island's history as a place of exile for disgraced officials.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Mission Hills</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">A massive complex with 10 golf courses designed by name-brand pros, hot springs with 168 pools themed after different countries, and a film studio backlot with streets modeled after 1930s Shanghai. Even if you don't golf, the hot springs alone are worth the trip — especially the火山温泉 section built with actual volcanic rocks.</p>
 </div>

 <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">🗺️ Beyond the Two Main Cities</h3>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Boao</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">A small coastal town that punches above its weight. It hosts the annual Boao Forum for Asia (think Asia's Davos) where global leaders gather in a setting that feels more like a beach retreat than a conference center. The coastal road between Boao and the nearby Tanmen fishing village is one of the finest drives in Hainan — cliffs dropping to the ocean on one side, forested hills on the other. The town itself is quiet and walkable, with a pleasant boardwalk along the estuary. If you're driving between Haikou and Sanya, stopping here for lunch and a stroll breaks up the trip beautifully.</p>
 <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-400 p-3 rounded-r-lg text-sm text-blue-800">
 💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> The Boao Lecheng medical zone next door is China's only special zone where overseas-approved drugs and therapies are available before they get mainland approval. If you're pursuing a specific treatment, it's worth researching whether it's available here — some travelers combine a procedure with a resort recovery stay.
 </div>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wuzhishan (Five Finger Mountain)</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The best hiking on the island — five peaks rising out of the jungle like fingers, with cloud forest near the top and panoramic views when the weather clears. The climb is no joke (3-4 hours up), but the reward is being above the mist looking over an island that feels more like Jurassic Park than China. Go on a clear weekday if you can.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Xinglong</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">A weird and wonderful town shaped by returned overseas Chinese from Southeast Asia who brought back coffee culture in the 1950s. The tropical botanical garden is the main draw: acres of spice plants, cocoa trees, and the original coffee bushes that started Hainan's coffee scene. The coffee itself is decent — try the local style with condensed milk.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Bawangling</h4>
 <p class="text-gray-700">This rainforest is home to the Hainan gibbon — the rarest primate on Earth with fewer than 40 individuals left. You'd need serious luck (and a guide) to spot one, but the forest itself is worth the trip: towering trees, wild orchids, and a silence that feels different from anywhere else in Hainan. A day trip from Haikou if you're willing to drive.</p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <!-- Getting Around -->
 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="getting-around" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">🚇 Getting Around Hainan</h2>
 <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-700">
 <li><strong>High-speed rail:</strong> Hainan has a circular high-speed rail line that connects Haikou, Boao, Sanya, and most towns. Trains run frequently and are cheap (Haikou → Sanya ~¥100, 1.5 hours).</li>
 <li><strong>Taxis / Didi:</strong> Call a ride easily through the <a href="/fr/transport/didi/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">DiDi app</a> or via Alipay/WeChat mini programs.</li>
 <li><strong>Car rental:</strong> Possible with an International Driving Permit + Chinese translation</li>
 <li><strong>Buses:</strong> Cheap but slow, routes cover most tourist spots</li>
 </ul>
 </section>

