 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="where-to-stay" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">🏨 Where to Stay</h2>
 <p class="text-gray-700 mb-6">Hainan resorts are generally cheaper than equivalent quality in Southeast Asia. A ¥500/night room in Yalong Bay often includes breakfast and has a private beach area.</p>

 <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">💰 Budget (¥100-200)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Dadonghai area hostels (Sanya) · Qilou area guesthouses (Haikou)</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">💰💰 Mid-Range (¥300-600)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Yalong Bay mid-range resorts (Sanya) · Mission Hills area hotels (Haikou)</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
 <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">💰💰💰 Premium (¥800+)</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm">Atlantis, Edition, Ritz-Carlton — Haitang Bay (Sanya) · Hilton, Marriott city center (Haikou)</p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </section>

 <!-- What to Eat -->
 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="what-to-eat" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">🍜 What to Eat in Hainan</h2>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Hainan's food is all about fresh tropical flavors — lots of seafood, coconut, and rice.</p>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wenchang Chicken</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Hainan's signature dish — poached free-range chicken with silky skin and subtly sweet meat. The ginger-scallion oil dipping sauce is the real star, bright and punchy. The best version is in Wenchang city itself (an hour from Haikou), where they serve it simply as a cold appetizer with chicken-rice soup on the side. You haven't truly tasted Hainan until you've had this.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Jiaji Duck</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The unsung hero of Hainanese cuisine — duck slow-cooked until the meat falls off the bone and the broth turns into a rich, amber-colored liquid. The cooking is deceptively simple: duck, water, salt, and Chinese herbs for a subtle anise-and-ginger warmth. Dip the meat in fermented bean paste and chili for the full effect. It's Hainanese home cooking at its purest, best found in the Jiaji area near Boao.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Hainan Noodles</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">The breakfast that fuels the island — thin rice noodles in a light broth made from pork bones, dried shrimp, and squid, topped with fried garlic and scallions. Two styles to try: <em>tang mian</em> (soup version, the classic morning bowl) and <em>ban mian</em> (dry style with a thick savory sauce). Add a spoonful of local chili paste and you'll understand why every Hainanese person starts their day this way.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Seafood Market Experience</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">An afternoon activity in itself — head to Sanya's seafood market, pick your live catch from tanks of mantis shrimp, flower crabs, and grouper, then take it upstairs to have it cooked on the spot. Grilled with garlic and chili, or steamed with ginger and scallion. About ¥100-150 per person for a feast, and the mantis shrimp alone are worth the trip — they have a sweetness frozen imports can't touch.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Coconut Rice</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Sticky rice steamed inside a fresh coconut with coconut milk and pandan — fragrant, slightly sweet, almost pudding-like. The outer layer caramelizes into a delicate crust, and the best versions include shredded coconut meat mixed right in. Street vendors sell it wrapped in banana leaves — look for it at the Wenchang night market.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
 <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Qingbuliang</h3>
 <p class="text-gray-700">Hainan's answer to the tropical heat — a shaved-ice bowl of coconut milk with chewy taro, red beans, watermelon, aloe vera jelly, and seaweed-starch "noodles" that slide down in the most satisfying way. Sweet but not cloying, creamy but light. Every stall has their own spin (some add ice cream, others crushed peanuts). About ¥10 a bowl, best enjoyed at Sanya's waterfront night market when the humidity hits.</p>
 </div>
 </section>

 <!-- Visa-Free Rules -->
 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="visa-rules" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Visa-Free Rules — Exactly What You Need to Know</h2>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl border border-gray-200 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
 <table class="w-full text-sm">
 <thead>
 <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
 <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-gray-700">Question</th>
 <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-gray-700">Answer</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">How long can I stay?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3">Up to <strong>30 days</strong> — counted from the day after you arrive</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">Do I need a tour group?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3"><strong>No</strong> — independent solo travelers are fine</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">Can I extend?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3">Possibly, apply at the local immigration office in Hainan</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">Can I visit mainland China?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3"><strong>No</strong> — this policy only covers Hainan island</td>
 </tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">Can I enter Hainan, go to Hong Kong, then come back?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3">Yes — each entry resets the 30-day clock</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium">What if I overstay?</td>
 <td class="px-5 py-3">Fines + possible entry ban. Don't risk it.</td>
 </tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <p class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Entry requirements:</p>
 <ol class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1.5 text-gray-700">
 <li>✅ Passport valid for 6+ months</li>
 <li>✅ Return or onward ticket within 30 days</li>
 <li>✅ Hotel booking (recommended)</li>
 <li>✅ Arrival card (given on the plane or <a href="/fr/visa/arrival-card/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">fill online</a>)</li>
 </ol>
 </section>

 <!-- Practical Tips -->
 <section class="mb-12">
 <h2 id="tips" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Practical Tips for Hainan</h2>
 <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700">
 <li><strong>Pay with Alipay or WeChat Pay</strong> — set them up before you fly. Most places in Hainan are cashless. See our <a href="/fr/payment/payment-guide/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Comment payer en Chine</a> guide.</li>
 <li><strong>VPN is still needed</strong> — Google, WhatsApp, Instagram won't work without it. <a href="/fr/life/best-vpns/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Set up a VPN</a> before you arrive.</li>
 <li><strong>SIM card / eSIM</strong> — buy one online (airalo, Holafly) or at the airport. See our <a href="/fr/life/best-sim-esim/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">SIM guide</a>.</li>
 <li><strong>Language</strong> — English is not widely spoken in Hainan outside of resorts. Have Google Translate or Pleco ready.</li>
 <li><strong>Sunscreen + mosquito repellent</strong> — both are essentials year-round.</li>
 <li><strong>Tipping</strong> — not expected. See our <a href="/fr/payment/tipping/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">tipping guide</a>.</li>
 </ol>
 </section>

